rail.prompt:
Here is a list of technical debts that I want you to identify within the provided code snippets.  
1. Long method
2. Deeply nested control flow statements (e.g., if, for, while, switch, try)
3. Throw generic exceptions
4. Large class
Below, you will find the explanations for each type of the technical debts along with sample code snippets.
Each example pair consists of a "Bad Example" that demonstrates the technical debt, and a "Good Example" where the technical debt has been resolved.

######################################################
Technical Debt 1. Long Method
Explanation: A "Long Method" refers to a method that is excessively lengthy or complex. Such methods often violate the Single Responsibility Principle, as they try to handle multiple tasks. This can lead to code that is difficult to understand, maintain, and test. Breaking down long methods into smaller, more focused ones enhances code readability and makes the codebase easier to manage and modify.

Bad Example 1.1: Long Method

public class UserManager {
    public void ProcessUser(int userId) {
        if (userId <= 0) {
            Console.WriteLine("Invalid user ID provided.");
            return;
        }

        string userData = LoadUserData(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            Console.WriteLine("No user found with ID: " + userId);
            return;
        }

        List<Order> orders = GetUserOrders(userId);
        if (orders.Count == 0) {
            Console.WriteLine("User has no orders.");
            return;
        }

        foreach (Order order in orders) {
            if (order.IsDelivered) {
                Console.WriteLine("Order " + order.Id + " already delivered.");
            } else {
                ProcessOrder(order);
                Console.WriteLine("Processed order: " + order.Id);
            }
        }

        UpdateUserActivityLog(userId, "Processed orders.");
    }

    private string LoadUserData(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns user data as string or null if not found
        return "UserData";
    }

    private List<Order> GetUserOrders(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns a list of user orders
        return new List<Order>();
    }

    private void ProcessOrder(Order order) {
        // Assume this processes the order
    }

    private void UpdateUserActivityLog(int userId, string activity) {
        // Log user activity
    }
}

- Issue: The method handles multiple tasks such as user validation, loading user data, and processing user orders all in one. This makes it long and complex which makes it hard to maintain.

Good Example 1.2: Short method

public class UserManager {
    public void ProcessUser(int userId) {
        if (!ValidateUserId(userId)) {
            return;
        }

        string userData = LoadUserData(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            Console.WriteLine("No user found with ID: " + userId);
            return;
        }

        ProcessUserOrders(userId);
        UpdateUserActivityLog(userId, "Processed orders.");
    }

    private bool ValidateUserId(int userId) {
        if (userId <= 0) {
            Console.WriteLine("Invalid user ID provided.");
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private string LoadUserData(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns user data or null if not found
        return "UserData";
    }

    private void ProcessUserOrders(int userId) {
        List<Order> orders = GetUserOrders(userId);
        if (orders.Count == 0) {
            Console.WriteLine("User has no orders.");
            return;
        }

        foreach (Order order in orders) {
            ProcessOrder(order);
        }
    }

    private List<Order> GetUserOrders(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns a list of user orders
        return new List<Order>();
    }

    private void ProcessOrder(Order order) {
        if (order.IsDelivered) {
            Console.WriteLine("Order " + order.Id + " already delivered.");
        } else {
            Console.WriteLine("Processed order: " + order.Id);
            // Assume this processes the order
        }
    }

    private void UpdateUserActivityLog(int userId, string activity) {
        // Log user activity
    }
}

- Benefit: The long method is broken down into smaller methods, each handling a specific part of the process. This improves readability and makes the code easier to maintain.

######################################################
Technical Debt 2. Deeply nested control flow statements (e.g., if, for, while, switch, try)
Explanation: It refers to situations where control structures like loops or conditional statements (e.g., for, while, if-else) are placed within one another, often to several levels of depth. It occurs when the code has multiple layers of nested conditions or loops (e.g., an if inside a for loop, which is inside another if statement, etc.).This creates complex logic flows that can be challenging to follow, making the code difficult to read, maintain, and debug. Having a series of control flow statements does not cause this type of technical debt if they are not nested within each other to a deep or excessive level. However, excessive nesting introduces unnecessary complexity. By minimizing the depth of nested conditions or loops, you can improve code readability, reduce maintenance effort, and lower the risk of introducing bugs.

Bad Example 2.1: Deeply nested control flow statements

public class NestedExample
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int x = 10;
        if (x > 0)
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < x; i++)
            {
                if (i % 2 == 0)
                {
                    try
                    {
                        Console.WriteLine("Even index: " + i);
                    }
                    catch (Exception e)
                    {
                        Console.WriteLine(e.ToString());
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

- Issue: The code contains overly deep nesting of control flow statements, making it difficult to read and maintain. This complexity should be reduced by breaking the code into multiple methods.

Good Example 2.2: Minimal nested control flow statements

public class MinimalNestedExample
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int x = 10;
        ProcessNumbers(x);
    }

    private static void ProcessNumbers(int x)
    {
        if (x <= 0) return;
        for (int i = 0; i < x; i++)
        {
            if (i % 2 == 0) PrintEvenIndex(i);
        }
    }

    private static void PrintEvenIndex(int index)
    {
        try
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Even index: " + index);
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error printing even index: {index}");
            Console.WriteLine(e.ToString());
        }
    }
}

- Benefit: This code minimizes nesting by utilizing separate methods for specific tasks, significantly improving readability and maintainability.

Bad Example 2.3: Deeply nested control flow statements

public class DeeplyNested
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int x = 10, y = 20;
        if (x > 5)
        {
            if (y < 25)
            {
                for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
                {
                    if (i % 2 == 0)
                    {
                        Console.WriteLine("Nested level 4: " + i);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

- Issue: The code demonstrates excessive depth in nesting control flow statements, which complicates understanding and maintenance. Refactoring to reduce nesting is advised.

Good Example 2.4: Minimal nested control flow statements

public class MinimalNestedExample
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int x = 10, y = 20;
        if (x > 5 && y < 25)
        {
            PrintEvenNumbersUpTo(10);
        }
    }

    private static void PrintEvenNumbersUpTo(int max)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < max; i++)
        {
            if (i % 2 == 0) Console.WriteLine("Even number: " + i);
        }
    }
}

- Benefit: By consolidating conditions and extracting repetitive logic into a separate method, this code reduces complexity and enhances clarity, making it easier to follow and modify.

######################################################
Technical Debt 3. Throw generic exceptions
Explanation: It refers to using broad, non-specific exception types (such as Exception) in error handling, instead of more specific, descriptive exceptions. This approach makes it harder to diagnose and handle errors effectively, as it doesn't provide enough information about the exact nature of the problem. Using specific exceptions (e.g., FileNotFoundException, InvalidOperationException) allows developers to pinpoint the root cause of an issue more accurately and handle errors in a more targeted way. This leads to more robust, maintainable code and improves the overall reliability of the application by making error handling clearer and more precise.

Bad Example 3.1: Throw Generic Exceptions

public class GenericExceptions {
    public static void Process(int value) {
        if (value < 10) {
            throw new Exception("Value too low");
        }
        Console.WriteLine("Value is sufficient");
    }
}


- Issue: Uses a generic 'Exception', which masks specific issues and complicates error handling.

Good Example 3.2: Throw Specific Exceptions

public class GenericExceptions {
    public static void Process(int value) {
        if (value < 10) {
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("value", "Value too low");
        }
        Console.WriteLine("Value is sufficient");
    }
}


- Benefit: Using 'IllegalArgumentException' clarifies the nature of the error and aids in precise error handling.

Bad Example 3.3: Throw Generic Exceptions

public class GenericExceptionExample {
    public static void Divide(int num, int num2) {
        if (num2 == 0) {
            throw new Exception("Cannot divide by zero");
        }
        int result = num / num2;  // Perform the division
        Console.WriteLine("Result of division: " + result);
    }
}


- Issue: Uses a generic 'Exception', which masks specific issues and complicates error handling.

Good Example 3.4: Throw Specific Exceptions

public class GenericExceptionExample {
    public static void Divide(int num, int num2) {
        if (num2 == 0) {
            // Throwing a more specific exception to avoid technical debt
            throw new DivideByZeroException("Cannot divide by zero");
        }
        int result = num / num2;  // Perform the division
        Console.WriteLine("Result of division: " + result);
    }
}


- Benefit: 'ArithmeticException' specifically indicates division errors, enhancing clarity and error management.

######################################################
Technical Debt 4. Large Class
Explanation: A Large Class, also referred to as a God Class, or Brain Class, is a class that centralizes too much functionality. It often becomes overly complex, containing an excessive number of fields and methods, and performing too many responsibilities that should ideally be distributed across multiple smaller, more cohesive classes. Such classes violate the Single Responsibility Principle and make the codebase difficult to understand, maintain, and extend. Large classes increase the risk of bugs and reduce the overall readability and modularity of the software. As a class-level technical debt type, it pertains to the structure and organization of classes, rather than individual methods. To address this, Large classes should be refactored by extracting related responsibilities into smaller, well-defined classes and promote better object-oriented practices.

Bad Example 4.1: Large class

public class SystemManager
{
    private List<User> users;
    private List<Product> products;
    private List<Order> orders;

    // User-related methods
    public void AddUser(string name)
    {
        // logic to add user
    }

    public void RemoveUser(string name)
    {
        // logic to remove user
    }

    // Product-related methods
    public void AddProduct(string productName, double price)
    {
        // logic to add product
    }

    public void UpdateProductPrice(string productName, double newPrice)
    {
        // logic to update product price
    }

    // Order-related methods
    public void PlaceOrder(string userName, string productName)
    {
        // logic to place order
    }

    public void CancelOrder(string orderId)
    {
        // logic to cancel order
    }

    public void PrintSummary()
    {
        // logic to print a detailed summary of all users, products, and orders
    }
}

- Issue: The SystemManager class is a Large Class, as it manages too many responsibilities related to users, products, and orders. This leads to code that is hard to understand and maintain.

Good Example 4.2: No Large Class

public class UserManager
{
    private List<User> users;

    public void AddUser(string name)
    {
        // logic to add user
    }

    public void RemoveUser(string name)
    {
        // logic to remove user
    }
}

public class ProductManager
{
    private List<Product> products;

    public void AddProduct(string productName, double price)
    {
        // logic to add product
    }

    public void UpdateProductPrice(string productName, double newPrice)
    {
        // logic to update product price
    }
}

public class OrderManager
{
    private List<Order> orders;

    public void PlaceOrder(string userName, string productName)
    {
        // logic to place order
    }

    public void CancelOrder(string orderId)
    {
        // logic to cancel order
    }
}

- Benefit: Responsibilities of the SystemManager class have been split into three separate classes: UserManager, ProductManager, and OrderManager. This refactoring improves cohesion and makes each class easier to understand and maintain.

